---
// Tokens removed against cost booked as saved, for two independent readings of
// the same metric. The point is that the second dot is always about twice the
// first, in production and in the benchmark alike.
const MAX = 30;
const x = (v) => (v / MAX) * 100;
const rows = [
  { name: 'production fleet dashboard',    tokens: 10.1, cost: 19.4, ratio: '1.9x' },
  { name: 'recomputed from this benchmark', tokens: 9.7, cost: 24.7, ratio: '2.5x' },
];
const pitch = 76;
---
<figure class="fig">
  <p class="fig-head">tokens removed against cost booked as saved</p>
  <p class="fig-sub">percent, two independent readings of the same metric</p>
  <div class="fig-key">
    <span><svg width="12" height="12" aria-hidden="true"><circle class="m-blue" cx="6" cy="6" r="5"/></svg> tokens removed</span>
    <span><svg width="12" height="12" aria-hidden="true"><circle class="m-warm" cx="6" cy="6" r="5"/></svg> cost booked as saved</span>
  </div>
  <svg height="172" role="img" aria-labelledby="mr-t mr-d">
    <title id="mr-t">Both readings book about twice as much cost saved as tokens removed: 10.1% of tokens for 19.4% of cost in production, 9.7% for 24.7% in the benchmark.</title>
    <desc id="mr-d">Two connected pairs of dots on a shared percentage axis. In each pair the cost dot sits far to the right of the tokens dot.</desc>
    {rows.map((r, i) => (
      <g>
        <text class="t-name" x="0" y={14 + i * pitch}>{r.name}</text>
        <line class="grid" x1={`${x(r.tokens)}%`} x2={`${x(r.cost)}%`} y1={36 + i * pitch} y2={36 + i * pitch} stroke-width="2" />
        <circle class="m-blue ring" cx={`${x(r.tokens)}%`} cy={36 + i * pitch} r="5" />
        <circle class="m-warm ring" cx={`${x(r.cost)}%`} cy={36 + i * pitch} r="5" />
        <text class="t-val" x={`${x(r.tokens)}%`} y={58 + i * pitch} text-anchor="middle">{r.tokens}%</text>
        <text class="t-val" x={`${x(r.cost)}%`} y={58 + i * pitch} text-anchor="middle">{r.cost}%</text>
        <text class="t-note" x={`${x(r.cost) + 2}%`} y={40 + i * pitch}>{r.ratio}</text>
      </g>
    ))}
    <line class="grid" x1="0" x2="100%" y1="150.5" y2="150.5" />
    <text class="t-ax" x="0" y="166">0</text>
    <text class="t-ax" x="33.333%" y="166" text-anchor="middle">10%</text>
    <text class="t-ax" x="66.667%" y="166" text-anchor="middle">20%</text>
    <text class="t-ax" x="100%" y="166" text-anchor="end">30%</text>
  </svg>
  <figcaption>
    Both readings book roughly twice as much cost saved as tokens removed. The metric credits every
    removed token at a flat <b>$2.00</b> per million, and on this workload the cache-weighted price
    actually paid was <b>$0.79</b> per million, so the saving arrives already inflated, before anything
    is subtracted for the cache reuse the removal destroyed.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
